@@ -0,0 +1,98 @@
+# Composition buffer for text-input drivers (`pane.compose`)
+
+## Status
+
+Implemented; validated by `scripts/conformance/cases/compose.py` on Windows.
+
+## Context
+
+A text-input driver (dictation, or any producer whose sentence is still being revised as
+it arrives) can only reach a pane through keystrokes or through a program-to-program API.
+Keystrokes force the producer to commit a partly-correct hypothesis into the terminal and
+then repair it with backspaces. On a real dictation corpus of 1372 frames, 94.8% of the
+finals only extended the previous frame, but 5.0% rewrote words in the middle of the
+sentence and 0.2% shortened it: correction is not an edge case, it happens several times
+per sentence.
+
+The application already implements the shape such a driver needs — the IME composition
+path: `marked_text` is rendered at the cursor as a preedit, and committing clears it and
+writes the result to the PTY as a single write. Nothing outside the process can drive it,
+because `ImmGetContext` for another process's window returns NULL.
+
+## Evidence
+
+- `docs/runtime-control-api.md` — the method table has no way to express "hold this text
+  in a buffer and write it later"; `pane.prompt` and `pane.paste` write immediately.
+- [`view.rs`](../../../../nebula_app/src/gpui_shell/terminal/view.rs) — `marked_text`,
+  `marked_text_range`, `replace_and_mark_text_in_range` and `replace_text_in_range`
+  already implement the preedit lifecycle, including "clear the preedit, then write the
+  committed string once" and feeding the command-line mirror.
+- [`view/runtime.rs`](../../../../nebula_app/src/gpui_shell/terminal/view/runtime.rs) and
+  [`workspace/residency.rs`](../../../../nebula_app/src/gpui_shell/workspace/residency.rs)
+  — the existing pane input methods already resolve window/pane, enforce the remote-pane
+  rule and reply with a typed `action` result; the new method reuses that plumbing.
+- `tools/probe_ime.rs` and an out-of-process probe: `ImmGetContext` against another
+  process's window returns NULL, so an external tool cannot address that process's IME
+  context to set a composition string.
+
+## Decision
+
+Add one runtime method, `pane.compose`:
+
+- `text` with `commit=false` (default) sets or replaces the pane's composition string.
+  Nothing is written to the PTY; the text renders through the same path as an IME preedit.
+  An empty string cancels the composition.
+- `text` with `commit=true` clears the buffer and writes the whole string to the PTY as
+  one write — the path an IME uses for a committed result.
+- `text` passes the same byte boundary as `pane.paste`, and local content still may not be
+  sent automatically to a remote (SSH) pane.
+- The method is declared in `runtime.describe` (`pane.compose`, feature
+  `pane.compose.buffer`) so clients probe instead of guessing.
+
+## Rejected alternatives
+
+- **Streaming keystrokes plus backspace repairs** (what an external driver can do today):
+  intermediate states become real input side effects in someone else's window, every
+  repair competes with the target's input stack, and the half-corrected sentence is
+  visible in the terminal and in its shell history.
+- **Setting the target process's composition string from outside**: not possible — a
+  cross-process `ImmGetContext` returns NULL, so the composition must be created in the
+  target process.
+- **Being an IME (TSF text service)**: it would compose in-process, but it hijacks the
+  user's active input method for the duration, needs installation and registration, and is
+  far heavier than an API the application can expose about itself.
+- **A CLI verb (`pebrel pane compose`)**: the caller is a driver that calls this per frame,
+  not a model issuing one command per turn. The resource+verb surface stays for
+  discoverability; this method is deliberately protocol-only and documented as such.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- While a composition is set, that pane's key handling defers to the composition, exactly
+  as it does for an IME composition. Callers must commit or cancel, which is why cancel is
+  part of the contract rather than an afterthought.
+- `pane.compose` needs the GPUI shell for its rendering. The legacy shell returns
+  `unsupported_target` for `commit=false` instead of silently downgrading to a paste.
+- Composition is not a byte-injection bypass: the validation and the control-sequence
+  boundary are the same as `pane.paste`.
+
+## Validation
+
+- `cargo test -p nebula`: parse and validation tests in
+  [`runtime_api/tests.rs`](../../../../nebula_app/src/runtime_api/tests.rs) cover the
+  commit flag, empty-text cancel and the byte boundary.
+- `python scripts/conformance/run.py --case compose` against a freshly built Windows
+  binary: the composition stays off the Grid, rewriting leaves no trace, commit writes
+  the text exactly once, cancel leaves nothing behind, and a control sequence is rejected
+  without reaching the Grid.
+- Manual: composing into a live pane shows the preedit at the cursor; committing inserts
+  the final text once.
+
+## Supersedes
+
+None.
+
+## Revisit when
+
+A TSF text service lands (in-process composition for applications other than this one), or
+a second consumer needs composition semantics other than "replace the whole buffer, commit
+once".
